message: "If you use this code, please cite the paper below."
type: software
title: "arc-prize-2026: code and data for 'What should an agent carry from one level to the next?'"
authors:
  - family-names: Rodrigues
    given-names: Carson
repository-code: "https://github.com/rodriguescarson/arc-prize-2026"
url: "https://github.com/rodriguescarson/arc-prize-2026/blob/main/paper/level-transfer/paper/paper_full.pdf"
license: MIT-0
date-released: 2026-11-03
keywords:
  - ARC-AGI-3
  - ARC Prize 2026
  - LLM agents
  - agent memory
  - human replays
  - interactive reasoning
  - Kaggle
abstract: >-
  Code and experiment logs for an ARC Prize 2026 Paper Track entry that asks what an ARC-AGI-3 agent should carry
  from one level to the next: the plan it used or the mechanics it learned. Includes the human-replay experiment
  (340 sessions), the model-based explorer and its ablation, and the hidden-set draw records. The 340 packed human
  replay sessions under data/human_replays_packed are not covered by this licence; they keep the terms of the
  original ARC Prize release (see their NOTICE).
preferred-citation:
  type: generic
  title: "What should an agent carry from one level to the next?"
  authors:
    - family-names: Rodrigues
      given-names: Carson
  year: 2026
  url: "https://github.com/rodriguescarson/arc-prize-2026/blob/main/paper/level-transfer/paper/paper_full.pdf"
  publisher:
    name: "Kaggle"
  collection-title: "ARC Prize 2026 Paper Track"
  abstract: >-
    An ARC-AGI-3 agent that has solved level k can reuse on level k+1 either the plan it used or the mechanics it
    learned. The paper tests both on 340 human replay sessions, in an ablation of a model-based agent, and on paired
    hidden-set draws of an LLM harness.
